#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128888 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a962d5e-5cc9-530e-9f95-85c399af7e4b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Decoupling Substitution of a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "b0707ce1-a0fe-5749-9742-4768621332a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5605" :question/problem "Find the substitution that will decouple the following system of differential equations:\n\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's construct the matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nfor the given system:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A$.\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are\n$\\lambda_1=-3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\lambda_2=1$.\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.For $\\lambda_1 =-3$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$For $\\lambda_2 =1$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we have $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = E\\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n{x_{1}(t) = 4u_{1}(t); x_{2}(t) = 3u_{1}(t) + u_{2}(t)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128889 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cfc838b-2e21-5021-8afc-000d12acdf39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "58c1b360-e67c-5074-919b-18fb5dbab146" :question/math-academy-id "e-5606" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 6x_{1}(t) + 7x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t) - 4x_{2}(t)$.\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=3$.*" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nfor the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A$.\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n$\\lambda_1=-1 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\lambda_2=3$.\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.For $\\lambda_1 =-1$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$For $\\lambda_2 =3$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThus, the general solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}v_{1}e^{λ_{1}t} + c_{2}v_{2}e^{λ_{2}t} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128917 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16d1e71b-6eda-5c91-81a0-15a1aa979932" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution Given the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "c7bde1c8-25e8-5573-9ac4-feedd40e122f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5974" :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=2$, respectively.\nWhat is the general solution of the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$?" :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$, the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}e^{-t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cfc838b-2e21-5021-8afc-000d12acdf39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8a2dcc4-18cc-521a-b8d3-d669cb946726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126927} {:answer/id #uuid "2a25db02-e58c-542a-956a-c2a73e36f7c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126928} {:answer/id #uuid "47596c87-09f0-5b01-9a14-87bf3186dd5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t}, c_{1} ∈ R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126929} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc3b6b1-7fcb-5ce5-a245-6dc9cce63f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126930} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4c0e4e-d0e0-5cb0-b994-9251fcd1dab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t}, c_{1} ∈ R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cc3b6b1-7fcb-5ce5-a245-6dc9cce63f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126930} :answer-field/id #uuid "06b947d1-234f-5e61-b0dc-a041fca78a20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126926}] :question/id #uuid "1b78e718-c7bd-590c-b65a-fabb1ffe4981" :question/math-academy-id "q-324453" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + 3x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)$.\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1} =-2$ and $λ_{2} = 2$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16d1e71b-6eda-5c91-81a0-15a1aa979932" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution Given the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9826ade2-94b5-5484-a10f-1bb048af9571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{4t}c_{1} ∈ R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127929} {:answer/id #uuid "afda91cc-dadb-53b1-b66f-424d3a4aa321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{4t}, c_{1} ∈ R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127930} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c34480-d5f1-52ef-bee1-e7dce48daf9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{4t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R" :db/id #e